    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `check_server(server, cases)` in `mcpcheck/inproc.py`. It connects to the server in memory, with no subprocess or sockets, and returns the same mismatches in the same order as `check_command`. All 5 tests pass, including 3 new ones.\n\n**How it works.** The pinned SDK (mcp 2.2.0) has a public `mcp.Client` that can connect directly to a server object. I use it with two settings:\n- **`mode=\"legacy\"`** makes it do the same `initialize()` handshake as the stdio version, just over in-memory streams. The default mode would take a different route inside the SDK.\n- **`cache=None`** turns off the client's response cache.\n\nEach case's tool is called through `client.session.call_tool`, the same `ClientSession.call_tool` that `check_command` uses. The result goes through the same `compare()` in case order, so the matching rules are unchanged. It accepts a server built with the decorator API (`MCPServer` and `@server.tool()`) and also a low-level `Server`. No dependencies were added.\n\n**Tests (`tests/test_inproc.py`).** They use a small decorator-API server with `add`, `greet` and a `boom` tool that raises an error. They check:\n- Mismatches come back in case order with the right `got` text, and an all-passing set returns `[]`.\n- Running the same cases against the same server through `check_command` as a real stdio subprocess gives exactly the same list. This includes a tool that raises and a tool that doesn't exist, so error results match on both paths too.\n\nThe Context7 docs lookup hit its quota, so I read the installed SDK's source to confirm the API instead."
  },
